course: biology
lesson: 99
title: The penguin pool
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: On land penguins waddle like clumsy toys, but the moment they dive they shoot through the water like torpedoes, because their wings have become stiff flippers and their bodies are built for swimming.
explain: Not every penguin lives on ice: the penguins in many zoos come from warm coasts in South America and South Africa, where cold sea currents bring plenty of fish.
explain: A penguin's body is full of clever tricks: a black back and white belly to hide in the sea, feet that stay just above freezing, and a gland that sneezes out extra salt.
explain: Cartoons and Christmas cards put penguins next to polar bears, but the two never meet, because penguins live in the south and polar bears in the far north.
explain: The four penguins of Madagascar plot escapes like secret agents; real penguins do not plan, but they are curious and follow each other, which can look like teamwork.
fact: Penguins are slow on land but fast swimmers; gentoo penguins, the fastest, can swim at about 35 km per hour.
fact: Humboldt penguins live on the coasts of Peru and Chile, African penguins in South Africa, and the Galapagos penguin almost on the equator.
fact: Polar bears live only around the North Pole and penguins almost only in the southern half of the world, so they never meet in the wild.
fact: Keepers hand-feed penguins to know how much fish each eats, tell them apart by coloured flipper bands, and sometimes hide vitamin pills in the fish.
fact: Penguins are countershaded: from above the black back blends with the dark sea, and from below the white belly blends with the bright surface.
fact: Warm blood flowing to a penguin's feet passes cold blood coming back and warms it, so its feet stay just above freezing and little heat is lost.
fact: Penguins also keep warm with a thick layer of fat and tightly packed waterproof feathers.
fact: Adelie and gentoo penguins build nests of pebbles that keep eggs out of meltwater and mud; bare stones are scarce there, and they sometimes steal pebbles from their neighbours.
fact: Emperor penguin fathers keep the egg on their feet under a fold of skin for about two months of dark winter without eating, huddling and taking turns in the warm middle; emperor penguins find their partners and chicks by their calls.
fact: A gland above a penguin's eyes removes extra salt from its blood, and the salty drips run out of its beak; albatrosses and gulls have the same gland, but people do not, so drinking seawater makes a person thirstier and ill.
fact: Once a year penguins moult all their feathers in a few weeks; they cannot swim or eat then, so they fatten up beforehand and look scruffy.
fact: Many penguins are threatened by overfishing, oil spills, which make feathers lose their waterproofing, and warming seas; after a 2000 oil spill in South Africa, volunteers cleaned about 20 000 penguins.
fact: The penguins in Madagascar are four, but real penguins live in big colonies, sometimes of thousands.
exercise: Why are penguins so fast in water but clumsy on land? | Their bodies and stiff flippers are built for swimming | They are tired after swimming | Their feet are far too big and heavy to walk on properly | They walk only when they are ill
exercise: How fast can a gentoo penguin swim? | About 35 km per hour | Only about 3 km per hour | About 350 km per hour | Only about 1 km per hour
exercise: Where do Humboldt penguins live? | On the coasts of Peru and Chile | On the ice of the Arctic | In the snowy pine forests of northern Canada | On the ice of Greenland
exercise: Why can penguins live on some warm coasts? | Cold sea currents bring lots of fish there | Penguins prefer hot sand | They swim there only for short holidays in summer | The warm water has no sharks
exercise: Do polar bears eat penguins in the wild? | No, they live at opposite ends of the world | Yes, penguins are their main food on the sea ice | Yes, but only in winter | No, polar bears are afraid of penguins
exercise: Why does the keeper feed each penguin by hand? | To know exactly how much each one eats | Zoo penguins cannot catch fish | To teach them to talk | To keep the fish warm
exercise: How do keepers tell the penguins apart? | Coloured flipper bands | Their different voices only | Little name tags hung round their necks | Bright paint on their beaks
exercise: Why is a penguin black on the back and white on the belly? | To blend in from above and from below in the sea | To look smart like a waiter in a posh restaurant | To keep cool in the sun | Because its feathers fade underneath
exercise: Why don't a penguin's feet freeze on the ice? | Warm and cold blood pass each other and swap heat | Its feet are made of solid fat with no bones in them | Its feet have no blood in them | It never touches the ice
exercise: What do some penguins build their nests from? | Pebbles | Twigs and leaves | Soft snow | Seaweed and mud
exercise: Who keeps the emperor penguin's egg warm in winter? | The father, on his feet | The mother, inside her beak | No one, it lies on the ice | The oldest chick in the group
exercise: How do emperor penguins share warmth fairly in a huddle? | They shuffle so each takes a turn in the middle | The biggest stays in the middle | They take turns standing alone outside in the wind | They light a fire of pebbles
exercise: Why do penguins sneeze salty drips? | A gland removes extra salt from their blood | They have a cold from swimming in the icy water | They are allergic to fish | They are blowing bubbles
exercise: Why does a moulting penguin stand in a corner and not swim? | It has lost its waterproof feathers | It is being punished by the others | It is guarding a nest | It does not like the water any more
exercise: Why do penguins eat extra food before the moult? | They cannot eat while moulting | To grow brand new beaks for summer | To get heavy enough to sink when they dive | To store food for their hungry friends
exercise: How does oil harm a penguin? | It makes the feathers lose their waterproofing | It makes the penguin float far too high to dive for fish | It turns the penguin black | It makes the fish taste better
exercise: How does overfishing harm penguins? | They have less food for their chicks | Fishing boats scare them into the Arctic | The nets keep them warm | It makes the sea too salty
test: the 4 penguins in Madagascar, how many flippers and feet altogether | 16 | 8 | 12 | 4
test: 6 emperor dads each with an egg on their feet. how many eggs off the ice | 6 | 12 | 3 | 24
test: In a Christmas card a polar bear and a penguin share a snowy hill. Where could you really see both on one day? | Only in a zoo, as they live at opposite poles | On any iceberg in the Arctic | In Antarctica in the middle of the long dark winter | Anywhere it snows
test: yo the sign says these penguins come from a warm country, thats a typo right | No, some penguins live on warm coasts with cold currents | Yes, all penguins live on ice | Yes, people moved them there from the ice long ago | No, penguins live in deserts
test: In Madagascar, Skipper's team plans a clever escape. What is true of real zoo penguins? | They are curious and follow each other, but do not make plans | They plan escapes every night | They can unlock the zoo doors with their beaks at night | They speak to each other in words
test: In Happy Feet, Mumble finds his partner by singing. Which part is close to real life? | Emperor penguins recognise each other by their calls | Penguins tap-dance to find partners | Penguins find their partners by smell alone, never by sound | Penguins never call to each other
test: A penguin swims over a dark sea and a seal looks up from below. Why is the penguin hard to see? | Its white belly blends with the bright surface | Its black belly hides it against the dark water below | It turns see-through underwater | It swims too deep to be seen
test: What would happen if you drank lots of seawater? | You would become thirstier and ill, as you have no salt gland | You would just sneeze all the extra salt out like a penguin does | Nothing, people can drink seawater | You would float better in the sea
test: Why would pebbles be precious in Antarctica? | Bare stones are scarce and keep eggs out of meltwater | Penguins eat them for food | They soak up the sun and keep the nest warm like a heater | Penguins use them as money
test: A scruffy penguin stands alone and will not swim. What is most likely? | It is moulting and has lost its waterproof coat | It is angry with the keeper | It has forgotten how to swim after a long time on land | It is too full of fish
test: How could people's fishing far away affect penguins? | Fewer fish means less food for penguin chicks | Penguins would get caught in sunshine | Penguins would grow too fat | It would not affect them at all
exam: Identify the principal reason for which penguins inhabiting temperate coasts are able to subsist there. | Cold ocean currents supply abundant fish to those coasts | Such penguins require no nourishment | The coasts are covered throughout the year by permanent ice | Polar bears are absent from those regions
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the distribution of penguins and polar bears? | Penguins are confined almost wholly to the south and polar bears to the north | Both species inhabit the Antarctic ice | Both species inhabit the Arctic ice | Polar bears prey extensively upon penguins
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the incubation of the emperor penguin egg. | The father maintains it upon his feet for approximately two months without feeding | The mother carries it within her beak throughout the long winter without feeding | The egg is buried beneath the snow until spring | The parents exchange it every day
exam: It is given that a colony of nine penguins is enumerated. Ascertain the aggregate number of flippers. | Eighteen | Nine | Twenty-seven | Thirty-six
